(2) One defibrillation after 3 minutes of standard chest compressions;  
(3) 3 minutes of optimal chest compressions following one defibrillation;  
(4) 3 minutes of standard chest compressions following one defibrillation.

The breakdown of the percentage of total transport volume carried by each carrier and their growth rates is as follows:  
Europe accounts for 40%, with a growth rate of 7.5%;  
Asia-Pacific accounts for 27%, with a growth rate of 5.7%;  
North America accounts for 17%, with a growth rate of 5.7%;  
The Middle East accounts for 8%, with a growth rate nearing 18%;  
Latin America and Africa account for 7%, with Latin America growing at 8.8% and Africa at 7.4%.

(1) Drain large volumes of pleural fluid with minimal changes in intrapleural pressure (normal pleural elastance, as seen in hepatic hydrothorax or congestive heart failure);  
(2) A relatively normal pressure curve followed by a rapid decline (lung entrapment);  
(3) Initially negative pressure that declines rapidly (atelectasis) (Figure 1).

(1) Demographic characteristics (i.e., age, sex, nursing home residence);  
(2) Comorbidities [e.g., neoplasm, COPD, HIV infection, diabetes mellitus, diabetes with blood glucose ≥250 mg/dL, renal disease, liver disease, alcohol and/or illicit drug use, cerebrovascular accident, and congestive heart failure];  
(3) Physical examination (i.e., body temperature, respiratory rate, heart rate, systolic blood pressure, and altered mental status—defined as new-onset disorientation, delirium, lethargy, stupor, or coma);  
(4) Laboratory tests (i.e., sodium, Pao₂, hematocrit, BUN, blood glucose, and arterial pH);  
(5) Chest X-ray (i.e., multilobar involvement, cavitation, and pleural effusion);  
(6) Severity of CAP [i.e., ICU admission and Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI) risk class].

(1) Improvement in cough and shortness of breath;  
(2) No fever for at least 8 hours;  
(3) Reduction in elevated white blood cell count (a decrease of 10% compared to the previous day's treatment);  
(4) Ability to eat and absorb adequate nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract.

